/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*-------------------------- File concurrent.dox ---------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_concurrent */
/** \defgroup theory_concurrent Theory of concurrency
 \ingroup area_concurrent

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/concurrent` realize, beyond the book,
 the structures that several threads change at once without a lock, or with
 the fewest locks: the queues between producers and consumers, the deque of
 work stealing, the union-find and the hash tables shared by threads, the
 relaxed priority queue, and the event count on which a thread sleeps until
 a condition holds. This page
 collects what the headers take for granted: the model of the threads and of
 the memory and the meaning of correct and of nonblocking (see
 \ref theory_concurrent_notation), the structures and the cost of their
 operations (see \ref theory_concurrent_real), and, for each family, the
 property on which its correctness rests and the argument behind its cost
 (from \ref theory_concurrent_queues to \ref theory_concurrent_wait). The
 pseudocode of each operation is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the paper that it follows, and with
 the order of memory of each access to a shared word written as a comment.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_concurrent_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>shared object</em> is a structure whose operations are called by
 several threads, each a sequence of calls; an operation takes time, from
 its <em>invocation</em> to its <em>response</em>, and two operations of two
 threads may overlap. A run of the object is <em>linearizable</em> if each
 operation seems to take effect at one instant between its invocation and
 its response, its <em>linearization point</em>, so that the responses are
 those of the sequential object on the operations taken in the order of
 their points (Herlihy and Wing); an object is linearizable if its runs
 are, and then a thread may reason on it as on the sequential structure of
 the other areas. The headers name the point of each operation, e.g., the
 compare-and-swap that claims a place, or the store that publishes an
 item.

 An implementation is <em>blocking</em> if a thread stopped in the middle of
 an operation (descheduled, or slow) may keep the others from completing
 theirs, as a thread that holds a lock does. It is <em>lock-free</em> if,
 whatever the threads that stop, some thread that keeps running completes an
 operation in a finite number of its own steps, i.e., the system as a whole
 makes progress, though a single thread may starve; it is
 <em>wait-free</em> if every thread that keeps running completes each of its
 operations in a finite number of its own steps (Herlihy). A loop that
 retries a compare-and-swap is lock-free, since the compare-and-swap of a
 thread fails only because that of another one succeeded, but it is not
 wait-free.

 The memory is that of C++, which the headers use through `std::atomic`
 (Boehm and Adve). A <em>data race</em> is a pair of accesses of two threads
 to one location, one of them a write, neither atomic and neither ordered
 before the other; a program with a data race has no defined meaning, and a
 shared word is therefore always an atomic. Each atomic access carries an
 <em>order</em>: a <em>relaxed</em> one is atomic and nothing more; a store
 with <em>release</em> and a load with <em>acquire</em> that reads its value
 <em>synchronize</em>, so that every write that the storing thread made
 before the store <em>happens before</em> every read that the loading thread
 makes after the load; a <em>sequentially consistent</em> access is acquire
 and release and, moreover, takes a place in a single total order \f$ S \f$
 of all such accesses, consistent with the order of each thread. The orders
 between threads come only from these: a relaxed access orders nothing, and
 two threads that write and then read two different words with release and
 acquire may both miss the write of the other, which only \f$ S \f$
 excludes.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the threads that use an object</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of a bounded queue, a power of 2, so that the index
         \f$ i \f$ is at the place \f$ i \bmod c \f$, a mask</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$, \f$ t \f$</td>
     <td>the head and the tail of a queue, or the top and the bottom
         \f$ b \f$ of a deque: integers that only grow, the items being
         those of \f$ [ h , t ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{CAS}( x , e , d ) \f$</td>
     <td>the compare-and-swap: if \f$ x = e \f$, then \f$ x \gets d \f$ and
         true, else false (and \f$ e \gets x \f$ in the code), one atomic
         read-modify-write</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ S \f$</td>
     <td>the total order of the sequentially consistent accesses and
         fences</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{seen} \f$</td>
     <td>the copy that a thread keeps of an index written by another one,
         read again only when the structure looks full or empty</td></tr>
 </table>

 Two more facts of the hardware decide the costs. A write to a line of cache
 takes it from the caches of the other cores, which then miss on it; two
 words written by two threads on one line (<em>false sharing</em>) make the
 line travel at each write, and the headers put each such word on a line of
 its own, often on a pair of lines, since the processors of x86 fetch the
 lines two at a time. A read-modify-write, e.g., a compare-and-swap, costs
 several times a plain access and orders the memory around it; a structure
 is fast when its common case has none. Finally, the <em>ABA problem</em>
 of a compare-and-swap on a pointer: a thread reads the top \f$ A \f$ of a
 stack and its successor \f$ B \f$, another thread pops \f$ A \f$ and
 \f$ B \f$ and pushes \f$ A \f$ again, and the compare-and-swap of the first
 thread from \f$ A \f$ to \f$ B \f$ succeeds and puts back on the stack a
 cell that is no longer there. The headers avoid it by construction rather
 than by counters: a stack whose cells are only pushed one at a time and
 taken all at once never compares a pointer read before a pop.

 \section theory_concurrent_real The structures and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The structures of the area, the threads they admit and the cost
          of their operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Structure</th><th>Threads</th><th>Progress</th>
     <th>Cost of an operation</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SPSCQueue</tt></td><td>one producer, one consumer</td>
     <td>wait-free</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, no read-modify-write</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MPSCQueue</tt></td><td>many producers, one consumer</td>
     <td>wait-free push but for the allocation of a block, lock-free
         creation of a producer</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized, no read-modify-write but per block</td>
     </tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MPSCInbox</tt></td><td>many producers, one consumer</td>
     <td>lock-free push, wait-free take</td>
     <td>one compare-and-swap per push, one exchange per take of
         \f$ k \f$ messages, plus \f$ O( k ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MPMCQueue</tt></td><td>many producers, many consumers</td>
     <td>lock-free but for a thread stopped between its claim and its
         publication</td>
     <td>one compare-and-swap per operation without contention</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>WorkStealingDeque</tt></td><td>one owner, many thieves</td>
     <td>wait-free push, lock-free take and steal</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized for the owner, a compare-and-swap only on
         the last item; one per steal</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ConcurrentUnionFind</tt></td><td>any</td><td>lock-free</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log_{2 + m / n} n ) \f$ amortized on one thread</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ConcurrentCuckooTable</tt></td><td>any</td>
     <td>blocking (a lock per region), lookups without locks for the
         keys and values read atomically</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized for the
         doublings</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ConcurrentFlatTable</tt></td><td>any</td>
     <td>wait-free lookups but across a growth, insertions blocking only
         during a growth</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized for the
         growths; no removals</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MultiQueue</tt></td><td>any, by a handle each</td>
     <td>blocking (a lock per queue, taken only at the first try)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log m ) \f$ amortized on a queue of \f$ m \f$ pairs,
         \f$ c / ( c - 1 ) \f$ draws expected; relaxed</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>EventCount</tt></td><td>any</td>
     <td>a waiter sleeps, a notifier never waits</td>
     <td>a fence and a read when nobody waits</td></tr>
 </table>

 The queues of one producer or of one consumer are cheaper than the general
 one because each index has a single writer: it is written by plain stores
 with release and read by loads with acquire, and no read-modify-write is
 needed, while many writers of one index must agree by a compare-and-swap,
 and contend on its line. The copies of the indices keep the two threads of
 `SPSCQueue` off each other's lines but when the queue looks full or empty,
 i.e., once per lap rather than once per item. The many producers of
 `MPSCQueue` are given one lane each, a queue of one producer and one
 consumer, so that they never contend, at the price of a consumer that
 visits the lanes in turn; `MPSCInbox` is the simplest of the three, one
 stack, and it suits the messages taken in bursts.

 \section theory_concurrent_queues Queues of producers and consumers

 <em>Lemma 1 (the queue of Lamport).</em> Let the producer write the item of
 the index \f$ t \f$ and then store \f$ t + 1 \f$ in the tail with release,
 and let the consumer load the tail with acquire, read the items of the
 indices below it, and then store the new head with release, which the
 producer loads with acquire before it reuses a place. Then the consumer
 reads each item whole, after it was written, and the producer never
 overwrites an item not yet read:
 \f[
   \mathrm{write}( A[ i ] ) \to \mathrm{read}( A[ i ] ) \to
   \mathrm{write}( A[ i + c ] ) \quad \forall i , \tag{1}
 \f]
 \f$ \to \f$ being the order of happening before.

 Indeed, the write of \f$ A[ i ] \f$ precedes in the producer the release
 store of a tail \f$ > i \f$, which the consumer reads by an acquire load
 before it reads \f$ A[ i ] \f$; the store and the load synchronize, and the
 first arrow of (1) follows. The second arrow is the same argument on the
 head, since the producer writes \f$ A[ i + c ] \f$ only after it has read a
 head \f$ > i \f$, i.e., \f$ t - h < c \f$. The indices only grow and are
 reduced by the mask, so that the comparisons \f$ t - h = c \f$ (full) and
 \f$ h = t \f$ (empty) hold also when the integers wrap around. A stale copy
 \f$ \mathit{seen} \f$ of the index of the other thread is never ahead of
 it, and therefore only makes the queue look fuller or emptier than it is,
 never the converse; this is why the copy is read again before the queue is
 declared full or empty. Each operation runs a constant number of steps,
 whatever the other thread does, and the queue is wait-free. The
 linearization point of a push is its store of the tail, and that of a pop
 its store of the head (or, if it finds the queue empty, its load of the
 tail).

 `MPSCQueue` applies Lemma 1 to each lane, whose block holds the count of
 its items in place of the tail; the link to the next block is stored with
 release after the block is full, and a block goes back to its producer on a
 stack only after the consumer has read all its items, so that a block is
 reused only when (1) holds for all its places. In steady state a lane
 cycles over \f$ \lceil m / B \rceil + 1 \f$ blocks for at most \f$ m \f$
 items held at once, and an item costs one store of the count. The
 consumer of `SPSCQueue` and of `MPSCQueue` waits a few pauses when it is
 right behind a streaming producer and finds only a few items (B-Queue): it
 then takes a batch at once, and the lines of the count and of the items
 travel once per batch instead of once per item, which would slow the
 producer down several times over.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the sequences of Vyukov).</em> In `MPMCQueue` the place of the
 index \f$ i \f$ holds the sequence \f$ i \f$ when it waits for the push of
 \f$ i \f$, \f$ i + 1 \f$ when it holds that item, and \f$ i + c \f$ when it
 waits for the push of \f$ i + c \f$; a producer claims the index \f$ p \f$
 by \f$ \mathrm{CAS}( t , p , p + 1 ) \f$ only after it has read the
 sequence \f$ p \f$, and publishes by storing \f$ p + 1 \f$ with release, and
 a consumer does the same on the head with \f$ q + 1 \f$ and \f$ q + c \f$.

 The compare-and-swap gives each index to one producer and to one consumer,
 and the sequence, written with release and read with acquire, carries the
 order (1) from the producer to the consumer and back. A producer that reads
 a sequence below \f$ p \f$ finds the item of \f$ p - c \f$ not yet taken,
 i.e., the queue full, and one that reads a sequence above \f$ p \f$ lost the
 index to another producer, and reads the tail again. A failure of the
 compare-and-swap means that another thread claimed the index, hence the
 queue makes progress; but a thread stopped between its compare-and-swap and
 its publication holds up the threads that come round to its place, and the
 queue is lock-free only when no thread stops there.

 `MPSCInbox` pushes cells on a stack of Treiber, by a loop of
 \f$ \mathrm{CAS}( \mathit{top} , \mathit{next} , \mathit{cell} ) \f$, and the
 consumer takes the whole stack by
 \f$ \mathrm{exchange}( \mathit{top} , \textbf{nil} ) \f$ and reverses it.
 The ABA problem needs a compare-and-swap whose expected value was read
 before the cell it names was popped and pushed again; here the only
 compare-and-swap is that of a push, whose expected value is the top read by
 the pusher and whose new cell is its own, and a cell is never popped alone,
 so that a success always links the new cell above the true top. The same
 argument holds for the stacks of the lanes of `MPSCQueue` and of the
 returns of `ConcurrentPoolResource`.

 \section theory_concurrent_steal Work stealing

 The deque of Chase and Lev serves the scheduling by work stealing: each
 worker pushes and takes its tasks at the bottom of its own deque, as on a
 stack, which keeps the tasks it made last, and their data, in its cache,
 and an idle worker steals the oldest task at the top of the deque of
 another, which is often the root of a large subtree of work. The owner
 writes the bottom \f$ b \f$ alone; the top \f$ t \f$ only grows, by a
 compare-and-swap of the thieves or of the owner, and the items are those of
 \f$ [ t , b ) \f$.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the last item).</em> Let `take()` store \f$ b - 1 \f$ in the
 bottom and then load the top, and `steal()` load the top and then the
 bottom, all four accesses sequentially consistent. Then, when one item is
 left, the owner and a thief do not both take it, and they do not both
 leave it.

 In fact, the four accesses lie in \f$ S \f$. If the load of the bottom of
 the thief follows in \f$ S \f$ the store of the owner, the thief sees
 \f$ t \ge b \f$ and gives up, or both race for the item by a
 compare-and-swap of \f$ t \f$ from the same value, which only one wins;
 otherwise the load of the top of the owner follows the load of the thief,
 and in either case the owner sees a top that tells it whether to race.
 With release and acquire alone both threads could read the old value of
 the word written by the other and both take the item; this is the reason
 of the two fences of Lê et al., written in the code as sequentially
 consistent accesses, which the sanitizer of threads models. All other
 items are taken by one side only, since a thief takes \f$ t \f$ and the
 owner \f$ b - 1 \f$, distinct when more than one item is left.

 A full vector is doubled by the owner, which copies the items of
 \f$ [ t , b ) \f$ to their indices in a vector of twice the places and
 publishes it with release; a thief that read the old vector reads from it
 an item that the copy did not change, and the old vectors are kept until
 the deque dies, so that no read is ever of freed memory. Their sizes form a
 geometric series, below the size of the last vector, and the doublings cost
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized per push, as in the vector of section 4.3.

 \section theory_concurrent_uf Union-find

 `ConcurrentUnionFind` keeps a forest of parents, each an atomic word, and
 changes it only by compare-and-swap (Jayanti and Tarjan).

 <em>Lemma 4 (linking by index).</em> If a root \f$ x \f$ is hooked only
 under a root \f$ y < x \f$, by \f$ \mathrm{CAS}( p[ x ] , x , y ) \f$, and a
 parent \f$ p[ x ] \f$ is otherwise only replaced by an ancestor of
 \f$ x \f$, then at every moment
 \f[
   p[ x ] \le x \quad \forall x , \tag{2}
 \f]
 the forest has no cycle, and the root of each tree is its least element.

 By induction on the steps: a hook succeeds only if \f$ x \f$ is still a
 root, and sets \f$ p[ x ] = y < x \f$; the splitting of `find()` replaces
 \f$ p[ x ] = u \f$ by \f$ p[ u ] \le u \f$, by a compare-and-swap that fails
 if \f$ p[ x ] \f$ changed meanwhile, so that the new parent is an ancestor
 and (2) still holds. Along a path the elements decrease strictly, which
 excludes a cycle and makes the root the least element; hence the label of a
 set does not depend on the order of the operations. A failed
 compare-and-swap of a splitting is harmless, since it only leaves a longer
 path, and a failed hook means that another thread hooked \f$ x \f$ first,
 which is progress; `unite()` then finds the roots again. The operations are
 linearizable: `unite()` at its successful hook, `find()` at the read that
 finds a root, and `sameSet()` at the second `find()` when the root of
 \f$ x \f$ is still a root then. On one thread the linking by index with the
 splitting costs \f$ O( \log_{2 + m / n} n ) \f$ amortized per operation on
 \f$ m \f$ operations and \f$ n \f$ elements (Tarjan and van Leeuwen); the
 randomized linking of Jayanti and Tarjan has a better bound on
 \f$ p \f$ threads, at the price of labels that are not the least
 elements.

 \section theory_concurrent_hash The hash table shared by threads

 `ConcurrentCuckooTable` is the cuckoo hashing of `BucketCuckooTable` (see
 the theory of dictionaries), with a lock, a counter of versions, per region
 of buckets; both buckets of a key, and every bucket of the cuckoo path of an
 insertion, lie in the region of the key, so that an operation on one key
 takes one lock. Three arguments make it correct.

 First, no deadlock. The operations that take more than one lock (the
 doubling, `reserve()`, `clear()`, `forEach()`, `consistent()`) take all
 those in use in increasing order, and a thread that holds the lock
 \f$ i \f$ and waits for \f$ j \f$ has \f$ i < j \f$; a cycle of waits would
 give \f$ i_1 < i_2 < \cdots < i_k < i_1 \f$, which is impossible.

 Second, the lookups without locks (a seqlock). A reader loads the version
 \f$ v \f$ with acquire, gives up if it is odd (a writer holds the lock),
 reads the tags and the places with relaxed atomic loads, issues an acquire
 fence and loads the version again: if it is still \f$ v \f$, no writer
 held the lock in between, and what it read is a state of the region. A
 writer makes the version odd by a compare-and-swap and issues a release
 fence before its relaxed stores, so that a reader that reads one of those
 stores reads, after its fence, the odd version or a later one, and tries
 again (Boehm). No access is a data race, since all of them are atomic.

 Third, the doubling. A region whose load would pass 0.98, or an insertion
 that finds no cuckoo path of at most 5 moves among 1024 buckets, doubles
 the table under all the locks; the tables replaced stay alive while a
 reader may be on one, and with sizes that double they occupy less than the
 table in use. With 2 buckets of 4 places, \f$ n \f$ random keys fit with
 high probability while \f$ n < 0.9803 \cdot 4 B \f$, the threshold of the
 4-orientability of the random graphs (Cain, Sanders and Wormald), and a
 search in breadth over \f$ M \f$ places finds a path of
 \f$ O( \log_4 M ) \f$ moves, against the \f$ M / 2 \f$ of a search in depth
 (Li et al.). A lookup touches two buckets, i.e., \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ lines, and
 an insertion \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected moves, the doublings adding
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized; with uniform keys two operations contend for a
 lock with probability \f$ 1 / P \f$ for \f$ P \f$ regions.

 \section theory_concurrent_flat Linear probing that grows

 `ConcurrentFlatTable` gives up what `ConcurrentCuckooTable` needs its
 versions for: a word, once written, never changes nor moves within its
 vector, since there are no removals and the keys are never displaced. The
 vector holds \f$ 2^l \f$ atomic words, each 0 or a pair (or the index of
 the node of a pair), and the key of hash \f$ h \f$ is looked for from the
 place \f$ \lfloor h / 2^{64 - l} \rfloor \f$ on, cyclically, up to its word
 or to a word 0; an insertion writes the first word 0 of that sequence by
 \f$ \mathrm{CAS}( W[ i ] , 0 , w ) \f$, and on a failure reads the word
 that another thread wrote there before it goes on. This is the folklore
 table that Maier, Sanders and Dementiev found the fastest of the tables
 shared by threads, with the growth and the nodes added.

 When the pairs lie in the words, each place has besides a tag, 0 or 7
 bits of the hash of its key with the high bit set, in a vector of bytes
 apart (the pairs in nodes have none, as a tag would add a miss to the two
 of a key found in a node), so that a lookup reads the
 tags of 8 places in one word and compares them with its own at once,
 reading the word of a place only when its tag matches; a key that is
 absent costs one line of the tags, \f$ 1/8 \f$ of the words or less, as
 in the tables with metadata of Swiss and F14 kind. A tag is written, with
 release, after the word it tags, and always with the same value, by the
 insertion that wrote the word or by any insertion that passes the place
 while it looks for its own; an insertion takes effect when the tag of its
 word is written.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the lookups without locks).</em> Let the words and the tags of
 a vector be written only from 0, once each, and only while it is in use,
 let an insertion write the tags of the places it passes before it writes
 its own, and let a lookup of \f$ k \f$ in the vector \f$ T \f$ read the tag
 0 at the place \f$ j \f$, after tags at the places from that of \f$ k \f$
 to \f$ j - 1 \f$ whose words hold other keys, and then find \f$ T \f$
 still in use. Then no insertion of \f$ k \f$ had taken effect when the
 lookup read the tag of \f$ j \f$.

 Indeed, an insertion of \f$ k \f$ writes the first word 0 of the sequence
 of \f$ k \f$, and before its own tag the tags of all the places it passed;
 had it taken effect, the place \f$ j \f$, which comes before its word or
 is its word, would have had its tag when the lookup read it, since a tag
 written stays. A lookup that finds \f$ k \f$ returns a pair that stays,
 and is linearized at the read of its word; one that finds the vector
 replaced starts over on the new one, which happens once per growth. The
 lookups read with acquire, so that the word of a tag read, and the pair
 of a node, built before the compare-and-swap with release that published
 its index, are read whole; they write nothing, and \f$ p \f$ threads that
 only read scale as \f$ p \f$ independent sequential tables. An insertion
 that finds \f$ k \f$ in a word whose tag is still 0 writes the tag before
 it answers, so that its answer, the key there, comes after the insertion
 has taken effect. For the pairs in nodes, which have no tags, the lemma
 holds with the words in place of the tags, an insertion taking effect at
 its compare-and-swap.

 <em>Lemma 6 (no insertion into a vector being copied).</em> Let a writer
 raise its counter and then read the flag \f$ g \f$, and the thread that
 grows the table raise \f$ g \f$ and then read the counters, all these
 accesses sequentially consistent, and let a writer that reads \f$ g \f$
 raised lower its counter and retry after the growth. Then no word of a
 vector is written once its copy has begun.

 The four accesses lie in the single order \f$ S \f$: if the read of
 \f$ g \f$ by the writer follows the raise of \f$ g \f$, the writer backs
 off; otherwise its raise of the counter precedes the read of the counters
 by the grower, which waits for it to drop, i.e., for the writer to leave
 the vector (the mutual exclusion of Dekker, which Dijkstra published).
 The copy reads each word of the old vector once and writes it at the
 first free place of its sequence in the new one, which nobody else reads
 yet; the new vector is published, with release, after all its blocks of
 4096 words are copied, and the threads that come to insert meanwhile
 copy blocks instead of waiting. With the load kept at most \f$ 1 / 2 \f$,
 a lookup reads on average \f$ \frac{1}{2} ( 1 + 1 / ( 1 - \alpha ) )
 \le 1.5 \f$ words when it succeeds and
 \f$ \frac{1}{2} ( 1 + 1 / ( 1 - \alpha )^2 ) \le 2.5 \f$ when it fails, at
 the load \f$ \alpha \f$ and with random hashes (Knuth), most of them on
 one line of 8 words; the copies of the growths cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 amortized per insertion, since the sizes double. An insertion is
 blocking only during a growth, and the lookups never are.

 A key and a value of 8 bytes each do not fit one word, and the pair of a
 node costs a second miss of the cache on each lookup that finds its key.
 Where the processor writes 16 bytes by one compare-and-swap without
 locks, a place is instead two words, the key and the value, written from
 \f$ ( 0 , 0 ) \f$ by that instruction (the folklore table of Maier et
 al.), and Lemma 5 holds unchanged for the key: a lookup that reads the key
 with acquire and then the value reads a value written with that key, or
 a later one of `update()`, which changes the value by the same
 instruction and never the key. The copy of a growth moves the two words
 at once, and the places of a vector are twice as many words, all on the
 line of their key.

 \section theory_concurrent_mq Relaxed priority queues

 A priority queue shared by \f$ p \f$ threads under one lock serializes
 them, and even the exact queue with the best combining serves one
 operation at a time. A <em>relaxed</em> priority queue returns instead a
 pair that need not be the least: the <em>rank</em> of a popped pair is
 1 plus the number of the pairs present with a smaller key, and its
 <em>rank error</em> the rank less 1, 0 for an exact queue. A search that
 takes its states from a relaxed OPEN expands a few states that an exact
 one would not, and in exchange its threads do not wait for one another.

 The MultiQueue keeps \f$ n \f$ sequential queues, each under its own lock:
 a push puts the pair in a random queue, and a pop takes the least pair of
 the better of two random queues. With \f$ n = c p \f$ queues, at most
 \f$ p - 1 \f$ held by other threads, a queue drawn at random is held with
 probability less than \f$ 1 / c \f$, and a lock is taken at the first try
 or after a new draw, in at most \f$ c / ( c - 1 ) \f$ draws on average, 2
 for the default \f$ c = 2 \f$, with no thread that waits for a lock.

 <em>Theorem 7 (Walzer and Williams).</em> Let the pairs be spread over
 \f$ n \f$ queues uniformly at random, and then popped one at a time, each
 from the queue whose least key is the \f$ j \f$-th of the \f$ n \f$ with
 a probability \f$ \sigma_j \f$, where the sums
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} = \sigma_1 + \cdots + \sigma_i \f$ satisfy
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} > i / n \f$ for \f$ i < n \f$. The rank error of a
 pop converges in distribution as the pops go on, and the mean of its
 limit is
 \f[
   E[ R ] = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{\sigma_{\le i} ( 1 - \sigma_{\le i} )}
   {\sigma_{\le i} - i / n} ; \tag{3}
 \f]
 for the better of two queues drawn with replacement,
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} = 1 - ( 1 - i / n )^2 \f$ and
 \f[
   E[ R ] = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \left( 2 - \frac{i}{n} \right)
   \left( 1 - \frac{i}{n} \right) = \frac{5}{6} n - 1 + \frac{1}{6 n} ,
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 and the maximum over \f$ \mathrm{poly}( n ) \f$ pops is
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ with high probability.

 The argument is about the queues ordered by their least keys. The
 \f$ i \f$ best queues hold, of the pairs that come next in the order of
 the keys, a share \f$ i / n \f$, since the pairs were spread at random,
 and a pop takes from them with the probability \f$ \sigma_{\le i} \f$;
 when \f$ \sigma_{\le i} > i / n \f$ they are emptied faster than their
 share, and the number of pairs that separate the \f$ i \f$-th least key
 from the \f$ ( i + 1 ) \f$-th is a geometric variable whose mean depends
 only on \f$ \sigma_{\le i} \f$ and \f$ i / n \f$; Walzer and Williams
 prove that the ranks of the least keys of the queues converge to sums of
 such independent variables, whence (3). With one draw,
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} = i / n \f$, the best queues are emptied only as fast
 as they are filled, and the rank error grows without bound; two draws
 that fall on the same queue are such a draw, and `MultiQueue` draws two
 distinct queues instead, for which
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} = 1 - ( n - i ) ( n - i - 1 ) / ( n ( n - 1 ) ) \f$ and
 \f$ \sigma_{\le i} - i / n = i ( n - i ) / ( n ( n - 1 ) ) \f$, so that (3)
 becomes
 \f[
   E[ R ] = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \sigma_{\le i} \, \frac{n - i - 1}{i} ,
   \tag{5}
 \f]
 a little less than (4): 4.63 against 5.69 for \f$ n = 8 \f$, 24.53
 against 25.67 for \f$ n = 32 \f$. The
 earlier bounds of Alistarh et al., \f$ O( n ) \f$ for the mean and
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ for the maximum, hold for pushes and pops mixed as
 well, but for a comparison and a pop done atomically; as Williams,
 Sanders and Dementiev, `MultiQueue` reads the least keys of the two queues
 without their locks, possibly stale, and in a run of many threads its
 rank errors are measured rather than bounded.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the buffers).</em> In each queue, let the deletion buffer
 \f$ D \f$ be empty only if the queue is, and let no pair of the insertion
 buffer \f$ I \f$ or of the heap \f$ H \f$ be less than one of \f$ D \f$.
 Then the least pair of \f$ D \f$ is the least of the queue, and the push
 and the pop of `MultiQueue::Handle` keep both properties.

 The first statement is the second property. A push of a pair less than
 the greatest of \f$ D \f$ puts it into \f$ D \f$, moving the greatest of a
 full \f$ D \f$ into \f$ I \f$, which is not less than the rest of
 \f$ D \f$; a greater pair goes into \f$ I \f$, or into \f$ D \f$ when
 \f$ I \f$ and \f$ H \f$ are empty; and the pairs of a full \f$ I \f$ go
 into \f$ H \f$, which changes neither property. A pop takes the least of
 \f$ D \f$ and, if \f$ D \f$ becomes empty, moves \f$ I \f$ into \f$ H \f$
 and the least pairs of \f$ H \f$ into \f$ D \f$. Each queue is therefore
 exact, the relaxation is only in the choice of the queue, and Theorem 7
 applies unchanged; the heap is touched by batches of 16 pairs, which
 costs \f$ O( \log m ) \f$ amortized per operation on a queue of \f$ m \f$
 pairs and keeps the lines of \f$ D \f$ and \f$ I \f$ in the cache of the
 thread. The stickiness of Williams, Sanders and Dementiev, the two queues
 kept for \f$ s \f$ operations, saves more misses; it has no theory yet,
 and their measures show rank errors that grow about linearly in
 \f$ s \f$.

 A pop that finds both queues empty scans all the queues, each under its
 lock: when no push runs at the same time, a scan that finds nothing
 proves the structure empty, which a parallel search needs to stop.

 \section theory_concurrent_wait Sleeping until a condition holds

 A thread that waits for an item could look again and again, which wastes a
 core, or sleep for fixed lengths, which delays the item. The event count of
 Reed and Kanodia lets it sleep until another thread signals that the
 condition may have become true, with no lock and with nearly no cost for
 the signal when nobody sleeps. A waiter announces itself and reads the
 epoch \f$ e \f$ in one read-modify-write, looks at the condition once more,
 and sleeps while the epoch is \f$ e \f$; a notifier makes the condition
 true, issues a fence, reads the number of the waiters, and only if it is
 positive advances the epoch and wakes them.

 <em>Lemma 9 (no lost wake-up).</em> Let the read-modify-write of the
 waiter be followed by a full fence, and the write that makes the condition
 true be followed by a full fence before the notifier reads the waiters.
 Then a waiter does not sleep through a notification of a condition that is
 true.

 Indeed, the two fences lie in \f$ S \f$. If that of the notifier comes
 first, the waiter, looking at the condition after its own fence, sees it
 true and does not sleep; if that of the waiter comes first, the notifier
 reads its announcement and advances the epoch, and the waiter either has
 not yet compared the epoch, and sees the new one, or sleeps on \f$ e \f$
 and is woken. On Linux the fence of the notifier is only one of the
 compiler, and that of the waiter a system call (`membarrier(2)`) that
 makes every running thread of the process run a full barrier, which orders
 the two as a pair of full fences (Ladan-Mozes, Lee and Vyukov); the
 waiter, about to sleep, pays the system call, and a notification with
 nobody waiting costs the read of a line that stays shared.

 Before it sleeps, a waiter may spin for a while, since a condition that
 becomes true within a few microseconds costs no system call then; the
 spinning itself is not free, as the loads and the failed compare-and-swaps
 of the threads that spin on one line keep moving it between their caches.
 The exponential backoff of Anderson (`Backoff.h`) runs \f$ 2^k \f$ pauses
 of the processor before the look \f$ k + 1 \f$, up to a bound \f$ S \f$,
 and from then on yields the processor to the system at each look: after
 \f$ k \f$ looks a waiter has spun \f$ 2^{k+1} - 1 \f$ pauses, so that
 the looks of \f$ p \f$ threads at one line thin out geometrically while
 the delay added to a wait of length \f$ t \f$ is less than \f$ t \f$
 (the last doubling at most doubles it), and a wait longer than
 \f$ 2^{S+1} \f$ pauses gives the processor back. `spinning()` tells when
 the spinning is over, which is the time to move to the event count above.

